<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03H1">Exercise <span data-tag="03H1">7.6.4.13</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and suppose we are given an equalizer diagram </p>
<div class="equation" id="03H2">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="03H2" href="/tag/03H2">7.82</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:half-equalizer-always} \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ Z \ar [r]^-{g} &amp;  Y \ar@ &lt;.4ex&gt;[r]^-{f_0} \ar@ &lt;-.4ex&gt;[r]_-{f_1} &amp;  X } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Show that, for every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the map of sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C,Z) \xrightarrow { [g] \circ } \operatorname{Eq}( \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C,Y) \rightrightarrows \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C, X) ) \]
</div>
<p> is surjective (though it is generally not injective). </p>
</article>
